  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 223 PM EDT Mon Sep 21 2026\n\nDrier air will continue to filter into northern Michigan this \nafternoon as Canadian high pressure builds into the Great Lakes.\nNortheast winds will remain breezy at times as cooler air \nsettles south across the region, continuing high swim risks \nacross portions of the Lake Huron shoreline through Tuesday. \nTonight, clearing skies and lighter winds allow the colder \nairmass to to drop temperatures into the 30s. As a result, frost\nhas the potential to develop across the typical colder interior\nlocations, with the greatest potential across eastern Upper \nwhere lows fall into the low to mid 30s. A Frost Advisory has \nbeen issued for eastern Upper for tonight into Tuesday morning, \nwhile frost development across interior northern Lower has more \nlimiting factors for development due clouds and elevated winds \npotentially lingering longer... However, if cloud cover and \nwinds decrease more than expected across northern Lower, then \nsome additional frost headlines would likely need to be added. \n\nHigh pressure will then remain the dominant feature through \nmuch of the week, resulting in a prolonged stretch of dry \nweather across northern Michigan. Mostly sunny to partly cloudy \nskies and seasonably cool temperatures are expected through \nThursday, with highs generally in the 60s. Temperatures will \ngradually moderate slightly late week as surface high pressure \nshifts east and weak return flow allows milder air back into the\nregion, with locations looking to reach the upper 60s to low \n70s Friday into the weekend. \n\nRain chances remain minimal through the period, with the dry \nairmass and persistent high pressure keeping better \nprecipitation chances west and south of the area. Some guidance \ncontinues to suggest a weak shortwave may lift out of the \nnorthern Plains late this weekend, potentially bringing a chance\nfor showers. However, confidence remains low given the limited \nmoisture available across the region and the potential of the \nwave weakening. As such, thinking northern Michigan should \nremain dry through the weekend.",
